## What It Is

The Python reliability family flags language-level habits that quietly trade a
checkable guarantee for a run-time or review-time gamble: type-checker and
linter suppressions added without the specific code that says *what* is being
silenced (`# type: ignore` with no `[code]`, bare `# noqa`, `# pylint:
disable`), error handling that swallows everything (`except:` / `except
Exception: pass`), namespace-polluting wildcard imports (`from x import *`),
stray `print()` debugging left in shipped code, and `Any` annotations that opt a
value out of the type system entirely. It also covers dynamic-execution
surfaces (`eval`/`exec`/`compile`, `os.system`, `shell=True`, `pickle`,
`yaml.load`) as PY-008 / PY-009 — the Python counterpart of the
TypeScript `dynamic-execution` family.

These are the Python analogues of the TypeScript escape hatches Anvil already
governs (`any`, `as any`, broad `eslint-disable`, `@ts-ignore`) and the Rust
ones (`unwrap`, `panic!`, blanket `unsafe`). Each is sometimes the right call —
which is why the ubiquitous-but-noisy ones (`print()`, `Any`) default to
**opt-in**, and the family leans on suppression-with-reason rather than
blocking.

## Why It's Harmful

- A blanket `# type: ignore` (no error code) silences *every* type error on the
  line forever, including ones introduced by a later edit — the code that was
  wrong stays hidden. `# type: ignore[assignment]` silences only the named
  failure and lets new ones surface.
- A bare `# noqa` disables all lint rules on the line; a typo or a genuine new
  defect on that line will never be reported again.
- `# pylint: disable` turns the linter off for a rule (or all rules) with no
  inline justification of why the warning is wrong here.
- A bare `except:` (or `except Exception: pass`) catches `KeyboardInterrupt`,
  `SystemExit`, `MemoryError`, and ordinary bugs alike, then discards them —
  the program limps on in an unknown state and the real fault is invisible.
- `from module import *` pulls an unknown set of names into the namespace,
  shadowing locals unpredictably and defeating static analysis of what is used.
- A shipped `print()` is unstructured output with no level, timestamp, or sink
  control; in a service it pollutes stdout and is invisible to the log pipeline.
- `Any` switches off type checking for the value it annotates and everything
  derived from it, so the type checker can no longer catch misuse downstream.

## The Spectrum

From most to least severe in practice: a bare `except:` that swallows control
signals and bugs, then a blanket `# type: ignore` / `# noqa` that hides errors
permanently, then `from x import *` namespace pollution, then `# pylint:
disable`, then the ubiquitous `print()` / `Any` (off by default, opt-in). The
detectors are line-oriented and exclude test files (`test_*.py`, `*_test.py`,
`tests/`, `conftest.py`) so test scaffolding does not produce noise.

## The Right Response

Add the specific code to the suppression so it silences only the real failure
(`# type: ignore[arg-type]`, `# noqa: E501`, `# pylint: disable=broad-except`);
catch the narrowest exception you can handle and re-raise or log the rest
(`except ValueError:` over bare `except:`); import the names you use explicitly
instead of `*`; use the `logging` module instead of `print()`; give the value a
real type instead of `Any`. When a pattern is genuinely correct, suppress the
finding with a reason: `# @anvil-ignore PY-004 -- top-level daemon guard, logs
and re-raises`.

## Detection Signals

- `# type: ignore` not immediately followed by an `[error-code]` (PY-001).
- `# noqa` not followed by `:` and a rule list (PY-002).
- `# pylint: disable` directives (PY-003).
- A bare `except:` clause, or `except <Exc>: pass` on one line (PY-004).
- `from <module> import *` wildcard imports (PY-005).
- `print(` calls in shipped source — opt-in (PY-006).
- `Any` used as a type annotation (`: Any`, `-> Any`, `[Any`) — opt-in (PY-007).

## Example

### Observed

```python
import os
from .config import *          # PY-005: wildcard import

def load_port(raw):            # type: ignore   # PY-001: no error code
    try:
        print(raw)             # PY-006: stray debug print
        return int(raw)
    except:                    # PY-004: bare except swallows everything
        pass
```

### What actually happened

The wildcard import makes it impossible to tell which names `config` actually
contributes; the blanket `# type: ignore` hides every type error on the
signature line including ones added later; the `print` leaks to stdout in
production; and the bare `except: pass` swallows `KeyboardInterrupt` and the
real `ValueError` alike, returning `None` silently so the caller fails far away.

### The fix

Import names explicitly (`from .config import PORT, HOST`); scope the ignore
(`# type: ignore[no-any-return]`); use `logging.debug(raw)`; catch the specific
error and act on it (`except ValueError: raise ConfigError(...) from None`).
